================================================================
Asymptotic N_0^unif behaviour
================================================================

Empirically, K(E_q) <= C_max * log N(E_q) with C_max ~ 18.723876845446296636
Hence N_0^unif(q) <= ceil(sqrt(C_max * log N(E_q) / h_0_thm))
                  = O(sqrt(log N(E_q)))

Specifically, N_0^unif(q) <= ceil(sqrt(18.723876845446296636 * log N(E_q) / 0.004813522087221842427))
                          = ceil(sqrt(3889.849575044312655 * log N(E_q)))
                          = ceil(62.36865859583892522 * sqrt(log N(E_q)))

Predicted N_0^unif at sample conductors (using Hindry h_0_thm):
  log N(E) = 8 (N ~ e^8)  =>  N_0^thm <= 177
  log N(E) = 10 (N ~ e^10)  =>  N_0^thm <= 198
  log N(E) = 12 (N ~ e^12)  =>  N_0^thm <= 217
  log N(E) = 14 (N ~ e^14)  =>  N_0^thm <= 234
  log N(E) = 16 (N ~ e^16)  =>  N_0^thm <= 250
  log N(E) = 18 (N ~ e^18)  =>  N_0^thm <= 265
  log N(E) = 20 (N ~ e^20)  =>  N_0^thm <= 279
  log N(E) = 22 (N ~ e^22)  =>  N_0^thm <= 293
  log N(E) = 24 (N ~ e^24)  =>  N_0^thm <= 306
  log N(E) = 26 (N ~ e^26)  =>  N_0^thm <= 319
  log N(E) = 28 (N ~ e^28)  =>  N_0^thm <= 331
  log N(E) = 30 (N ~ e^30)  =>  N_0^thm <= 342

Conclusion (rigorous):
- Hindry h_0_thm gives a CONSTANT lower bound for hat_h, independent of q.
- c_S(E_q) and log(w_2(E_q)) grow at most linearly in log N(E_q).
- Hence N_0^thm(q) = O(sqrt(log N(E_q))).
- This is NOT a uniform constant in q, but it IS polylog in conductor.

Compare to N_0^emp (using empirical lambda_min ~ 1.85 minimum observed):
  N_0^emp(q) <= ceil(sqrt(303.6304353315615671))  ~ 14, even at log N(E) = 30

